# Gandhi Ramasamy and Periyar Ramasamy
Tamil title: காந்தி இராமசாமியும் பெரியார் இராமசாமியும்

Omandur Ramasamy Reddiar has won; truth has won. Firmness and integrity have triumphed. The electorate refused to accommodate intrigue and scheming. Dust fell upon the hopes of those who planned to capture the seat of government and hunt for profit. Victory stood beside noble people who understood and upheld the dignity of democracy.

Omandur was an innocent, guileless villager, far removed from political gambling. He refused to become a merchant willing to sell the seal of sacrifice in the political marketplace. He reduced personal life and comfort to the barest measure and possessed an ascetic disposition. Rather than remain behind books and refuse to observe the country's condition, he believed it was his duty to do whatever good he could when given an opportunity to serve the people's welfare.

He was unlike dandified people who ask, “Why, and for what?” He knew village life over many years, not merely by associating with villagers but by living as one among them. He understood that the reason white rule had to end was not only the passionate ideal of native rule but the fundamental justice of good government. Unlike worthless men with one thing inside and another outside, he possessed the resolve to labour for society and bear the consequent suffering. He lived simply and had excellent qualities.

Unless told that he was Chief Minister, no one could infer from his figure, clothes or gait that he occupied so great an office. Omandur was a self-controlled Tamil.

Omandur is a tiny village in South Arcot district. Beside it lies a small garden surrounded by fields, with useful trees, green crops, fruit-bearing plants and spreading vines. Omandur lived there for many years in a house away from noise and spectacle, cultivating purity and truth within himself.

Among many people who obtain an opportunity to govern, circumstances commonly permit rumours that they found employment for six sons, placed a son-in-law in a great Mysore post, gave a brother-in-law a mica-mining concession or obtained government work for relatives by marriage. Omandur was not caught in such circumstances.

Many political leaders are accused even by enemies of coarse temperament, breaking promises and concealing aims. No enemy could lay such charges upon him. Unlike those who refused to enter Congress's fire of sacrifice, hunted selfish profit under white rule and bared their teeth for office when opportunity arrived, he served Congress and emerged after bathing in its sacrificial fire.

He had not travelled to America, spent years in English libraries or shared moonlit dinners with officials. He had associated deeply with working people: Kandan the woodcutter, Muniyan the cattle-driver, Duraisami the gardener and Varadan the field labourer.

It was such an Omandur who faced a contest. A storm blew to overturn his seat; elaborate preparations sought to eliminate him. He won with 112 votes, while Prakasam, who assembled an army, lost with only eighty-four.

Not only his friends but most of the country's people rejoice, express pride, praise and congratulate him. To us, however, this victory inspires more fear than joy. Why? We refuse to forget the truth amid delight at Omandur's victory. The truth is that opposition arose against such a good man and a conspiracy attacked someone so selfless. It reveals how many wicked people roam the country, and that properly causes terror and anxiety.

When every party praises a man as good and selfless, and neither his former actions nor present conduct contains a trace of self-interest, the mere possibility of opposition deserves more attention and worry than his defeat of it deserves celebration. Many may rejoice that those conspiring to overthrow the ministry bit the dust. To us, the conspiracy against a man esteemed by the people appears sorrowful and shameful.

Men of corrupt intelligence said a foul smell arose. Suppose someone replied that people with noses should smell the flower and determine whether it was fragrant jasmine, and another did so and declared the accusation false. We would not treat this as a happy event. We would grieve that dull minds existed capable of doubting jasmine's fragrance and deceivers capable of exploiting that dullness to malign the flower.

So it is with Omandur's victory. More than an occasion for joy, it is an event revealing that wicked people capable of conspiring against the very best exist in this country, making the heart tremble.

Some will ask why we panic and babble: are competition and election not natural, indeed defining features of democracy?

Under republican principle, competition between one political party and another is proper and necessary. But opposition to Omandur sprouted within his own party.

Some call even that ordinary. If a person had inferior qualities, internal competition could be considered both normal and justified. Yet people called Omandur stainless; even those with defective sight did not accuse him of deceit or self-interest. How could such a man face opposition, and how can it be dismissed as routine?

One may argue that although he was good and selfless and believed his policies correct, that did not forbid others from presenting alternatives. Was this contest conducted because opponents said Omandur introduced an improper programme and offered another more suited to the country, the time and cultural development? No. The sole question was whether Omandur or Prakasam should occupy the seat. It was not a contest between their programmes.

Here lies the subtlety. Many forget it in victory's excitement; others conceal it to avoid stirring pain; a few deny it lest their hearts become visible. But no honest person who reflects calmly will dismiss it.

A leader of the ruling party, accepted by the people as good, faced opposition without any publicly stated reason. His character was not questioned, his programme not attacked and no alternative proposed. Yet there was a contest—indeed a quarrel, with volleys of abuse and even conspiracy. Why? Both sides hesitate to say, although both hearts seethe. This spectacle remains clear amid all the pain.

Ask an opponent why Omandur was unacceptable and he draws out, “He... he...” Press for the reason and he stammers: “He is a good man, golden, honest; there is no fault in him... but...” Unable either to chew or swallow what remains, he suffers.

Even people unable to understand English said he did not know English. That was no reason. They advanced such meaningless claims because they lacked the courage to tell the truth. Why? They feared the public's eyes would open and expose the schemers' fraud.

When Achariyar faced opposition, Kamaraj and others publicly gave reasons: he had betrayed the August movement and was arrogant. When Andhra Kesari Prakasam faced opposition, people said he intended to abolish scientific industry through a plan to eliminate mills.

What reason was given for opposing Omandur? Neither the victors nor the defeated told the people. Had the contest occurred in a public forum, people might have learned something of the cause. Instead it took place behind locked doors in a hall occupied by two opposing ranks. Only the result was announced; the internal debate was withheld.

Had it been disclosed, the issue would have assumed another form. The public would have seen clearly that this was not merely a battle between Omandur and Prakasam but between two forces they represented. The subtlety was concealed very skilfully, though not completely. People understood enough to infer an internal cause behind so fierce a battle conducted without stated reason.

Once the result became known, a cry arose near the hall. Sir Dr P. Subbarayan reportedly said it melted his heart and felt like molten lead poured into his ears. Whether one calls it a shout, a slogan or a warning, it is acknowledged that people cried, “Down with the Brahmins!” after learning the outcome. The doctor also says those who shouted were Congressmen.

A Congress leadership election occurred with no openly formulated reason. Omandur won; Andhra Brahmin Prakasam lost; people then cried “Down with the Brahmins!” The subtlety is as plain as four plus four equals eight and eight plus two equals ten.

People outside believed Brahmins caused the internal contest, wanted the Brahmin plan defeated and concluded upon Omandur's victory that it had been defeated. Denied a vote in the contest, they sought a way to communicate their feeling and found it by raising that cry. That is the matter.

One may ask why pain remains when Omandur won. The contest was not conducted to remove a man of bad conduct or attack a policy. It concerned Brahmins and non-Brahmins, although Omandur had not made that issue a programme or behaved in any manner that could be condemned as excessive because of it.

He was a very good man and seasoned Congressman. Yet the moment suspicion arose that, because he was not a Brahmin, his government offered no reliable path for Brahmin dominance, a group possessed the will, courage, means and power to say: “What if he is good or belongs to Congress? Our people gain nothing from his presence. Why permit him to govern? Let us remove him and place someone else upon the seat.” Does this not reveal the country's present condition and cause pain?

They once said Panagal and Bobbili were traitors and enemies of Congress, and that was why they removed them. What reason existed for attacking Omandur? Those who worshipped and exalted him a year earlier had discovered no defect. Why did they wage war? The people grasped the subtlety. Because they did, they dared raise the cry even if it wounded the honourable man from Kumarakamangalam.

A group exists that thinks anyone, whatever his party, must be removed if he does not serve them or cultivate their dominance. They must install someone useful to them, exploit every opportunity, invent a publicly attractive reason for opposition and work openly or secretly until successful. Can knowledge of this truth fail to inspire anguish and fear?

How can the heart remain untroubled when magnanimous men boast that, if a person is not useful to them, they will call even a flower dry leaves and diminish its value; and when they retain dominance in the land of valiant Tamils even in an age when knowledge is meant to rule?

Omandur issued prohibitory orders, broke strikes and imprisoned many under Congress policy. Those whom that prescribed repression affected had reason to be angry with him and condemn the course of his ministry and party. Such condemnation remained just even if they lacked the strength to enforce it.

But those who vowed to remove Omandur were not the people deprived of rights, suppressed or harmed by his government's actions. Those applauding the scheme belonged to his own party. It was not Periyar Ramasamy or Jeevanandham who plotted his fall. Men dressed in *khadi* like Omandur, resident with him in Congress and elected to the legislature through the same yellow ballot box, launched the effort: Vaidyanatha Iyer and Varadachariar. Khasa Subba Rao prepared for battle; *Bharata Devi* editor Ramaratna Iyer fired arrows; *Hindustan* editor Narayana Iyengar wrote abuse and slander. Seeing all this, what else could people shout but “Down with the Brahmins”?

Would they pause then to wonder whether the refined heart of Radhabai's husband might be hurt? Their hearts boiled because Omandur had committed no wrong and possessed no visible fault, yet the Madurai Iyer, Triplicane Achariyar and Andhra Pantulu opposed him without reason. From that heat was born the slogan.

Dr Subbarayan asks whether people, especially Congressmen, should shout such things. The people will instead ask whether Congressmen should conspire to overthrow a good and honourable Congressman. The critic, however, is a minister responsible for the Police Department.

“That noise in my ear felt like molten lead being poured into it,” said the doctor. The people might answer: “When some Iyers, calling themselves members of his own party, worked without one honest reason to overthrow Omandur, it felt as though red-hot pieces of iron entered our eyes. Doctor, you will not understand that pain.” They refrain from speaking directly only because further angering the minister may bring down his authority upon them.

Whether they speak or not, why should Dr Subbarayan not reflect? He would then understand their words. Is his earache more momentous than the pain in the people's hearts?

> Even should the Himalaya fall,
> he would not depart a grain from honesty;
> the very form of calm,
> he came to rule through love.
> Ramasamy, Gandhi of Tamil Nadu,
> our Chief Minister,
> by the fragrance of spreading fame
> has again become party leader.

This celebratory verse appeared in a daily newspaper.

Fragrant fame belongs to Ramasamy, the Gandhi of Tamil Nadu. Yet some called that fragrance a stench, and they will do so again—not only to Ramasamy, Tamil Nadu's Gandhi, but to Ramasamy, father of the Dravidians.

Must one faction forever oppose while others continually labour for honesty's victory? Is this Tamil Nadu's peculiar character? Even when people remain within one organization, claim one flag and one party, and worthy men praise someone as Tamil Nadu's Gandhi, selfish intrigue and the bitter hostility of communal frenzy continue to attack him.

How, then, is Ramasamy inside Congress in a position superior to Dravidian Ramasamy? Must Congress Ramasamy continue to seethe within while Dravidian Ramasamy remains outside with a heart pierced by spears? Is this to remain the country's condition? Is there no path out of this painful state?

Brahmins inflamed hostility by calling Periyar a Brahmin-hater. Yet Tamil Nadu's Gandhi, who proudly rejoiced in calling Brahmins and non-Brahmins alike children of Bharat, faces opposition like Dravidian Ramasamy. Why? Are there no honest people here to ask? Why can they not perceive that whichever kind of Ramasamy he may be, opposition comes in equal measure from one particular quarter? When will they understand?

*Dravida Nadu*, 4 April 1948.

---NOTES---
The opening scan confirms the title and shows that the stray `ள்` before item number 32 was OCR debris; it was removed from both canonical Tamil copies before building this document.

“Gandhi Ramasamy” refers to Omandur P. Ramasamy Reddiar, described in the quoted celebratory verse as `தமிழகக் காந்தி`; “Periyar Ramasamy” refers to E. V. Ramasamy. The title's parallel is therefore retained rather than replacing either name with an explanatory paraphrase.

The article uses `பிராமணர் ஒழிக` as a reported 1948 political slogan. It is translated literally as “Down with the Brahmins” to preserve documentary fidelity, not as an endorsement. Terms concerning Brahmin/non-Brahmin politics and “communalism” retain the author's historical argumentative usage.

Several personal names are partly distorted by OCR. Identifications such as T. Prakasam, Khasa Subba Rao, Vaidyanatha Iyer, Varadachariar and P. Subbarayan follow clear contextual evidence; less certain honorific allusions are translated descriptively without silently rewriting the Tamil source.
